title: 'Loom — THE TAPESTRY: the woven history view + Loom-native weaving nomenclature'
summary: 'Founder flagged a big build item: an easy DISPLAY of the woven threads of changes (history view), open to new nomenclature ''if it makes sense for how we do everything in Loom''. Proposal: THE TAPESTRY — a tapestry is a picture woven from threads you read, the exact need. It''s the cockpit for Loom''s killer use case (many parallel branches), because git''s commit-graph view collapses under many branches while a weave gets MORE legible. Weaving vocabulary maps onto Loom: WARP (vertical) = symbols (lifelines you follow down through time); WEFT (horizontal) = branches; a PICK = one weft pass = a single change/Justification crossing a warp thread (replaces ''commit'' floating in a graph); a KNOT = a conflict-as-decision (violet); the SELVAGE = the live working edge (git HEAD); a BOLT = a release/tag; the FABRIC = consolidated history, THE TAPESTRY = the view of it. Three cockpit capabilities (from Cid''s panel): (1) follow one warp thread — see every branch that touched ^auth + who decided what, one hop not twenty git-log scans; (2) contention heatmap — color warp threads by # of open branches touching them (cold=auto-consolidate, hot=schedule a decision), steer the agent-branch fleet away from collisions before merge-day; (3) speculative weave / merge-preview — preview a subset''s weave, forecast clean/knot/dangling, zero bytes checked out. It IS render-by-projection (the graph-slice projector we shipped, extended over symbol×branch×time) and would live in the Suite/Conductor as Loom''s navigation surface. Not chrome — the navigation primitive the parallel-branch killer feature requires.'
